body:
Browser agents execute real actions: clicking buttons, filling forms, making purchases. Right now there's no enforcement layer between "the LLM decided to click Buy" and the click actually happening. The agent has the same permissions as the human, with none of the judgment.

Two concrete problems this creates:

1. **Scope creep during execution.** A browser agent tasked with "research flights" can navigate to a booking page and complete a purchase. Nothing in the current architecture prevents a research task from becoming a transaction.

2. **No audit trail for actions taken.** When something goes wrong (wrong item purchased, sensitive form submitted, data scraped from a page it shouldn't have accessed), there's no cryptographic proof of what the agent was authorized to do vs. what it actually did.

Here's what enforcement looks like with scoped delegation:

```typescript
import { createDelegation, governAction } from 'agent-passport-system'

// Human delegates: browse + read only, no forms, no purchases, 1 hour
const delegation = createDelegation({
  delegatedTo: agentPublicKey,
  delegatedBy: humanPrivateKey,
  scope: ['browse:read', 'browse:navigate'],  // no 'browse:submit', no 'commerce:purchase'
  expiresAt: new Date(Date.now() + 3600_000),
  spendLimit: 0
})

// Before every browser action, the governance layer checks scope
const result = await governAction(
  { type: 'browse:submit', target: 'checkout-form', url: currentPage },
  async (action) => browser.executeAction(action),
  { passport, delegation, privateKey }
)
// result.permitted === false (submit not in scope)
// result.receipt has the signed denial — proof the agent tried and was blocked
```

The enforcement happens before the action reaches the browser. Denied actions produce signed receipts (Ed25519) that prove what was attempted and why it was blocked. Permitted actions produce receipts proving the agent had authority.

`npm install agent-passport-system` (v1.36.2) — the SDK is open source, Apache-2.0. Gateway at gateway.aeoess.com provides hosted evaluation if you don't want to run enforcement locally.

This could integrate as a middleware layer in the controller — every action passes through governance before browser execution. The delegation scoping means different tasks get different permission sets without changing the agent's code.

comments:
@aeoess Yes — `intent: "read"|"write"` as a first-class plan field is the right shape, and it's the same pattern as our existing `authoritative` field: external verifiers should be able to evaluate the plan without parsing per-op semantics. Will add it to the plan schema.

The framing I'd carry forward: APS = runtime invariant (authorization), Tap = compile-time invariant (scope). Orthogonal, composable, not competing. The structural rule you named — "write-intent op without a write delegation in the receipt chain" — falls out for free once intent lives on the envelope rather than implied by op name.

On `tap-aps-example`: hold off until we publish the plan format v1 reference (current `.tap.json` is stable in practice but not yet documented as an external integration contract). I'll ping when that lands so the wrapper isn't built against a moving target.

@laithrw - I'll try to find some time next week to reproduce again and capture the logs. I unfortunately did not keep my custom instrumentation or detailed logs from the runs. However I can try with standard DEBUG logging for websockets.client, cdp_use.client, and browser_use and provide.

I have only experienced the issue with browerless via CDP using keepAlive=true. (ghcr.io/browserless/chromium). If I create a new browser for every request via CDP for browserless I have never hit this issue.

The keepAlive is the ideal solution as we waste a lot time time for each task starting the browser up and logging in to the target system (often 50% of the total runtime).

@zhangxiaoshuai98, thanks for reporting this. I tested this, reproducing it as closely to your conditions as I could. The default index-based click path clicks the target cleanly at both DPR=1 and DPR=2, and there are no JS-level devicePixelRatio reads in the codebase -- the only DPR source is Page.getLayoutMetrics().

Are you running vanilla browser-use, or a wrapper like browser-use-webui/a custom agent/a custom tool set? If it's a wrapper with a coordinate-based click tool that feeds pixels from a screenshot straight into click dispatch, that would explain what you're seeing -- screenshots come back at device pixels (2x on your display), but Input.dispatchMouseEvent takes CSS pixels, so the click lands at 2x the intended position. The fix for that would be related to the wrapper, not this codebase.

Can you please share DEBUG logs from a failing run, so I can check the Input.dispatchMouseEvent coordinates being sent?
